    description: `The city now known as Atlanta was surveyed in 1837 as "Terminus," a literal railroad junction at the end of the Western and Atlantic Railroad line where nothing existed but red Georgia clay and the beginning of ambition — a name so aggressively utilitarian that it lasted barely three years before residents renamed it "Marthasville" after Governor Wilson Lumpkin's daughter. In 1845, Western and Atlantic Railroad chief engineer J. Edgar Thomson proposed "Atlanta," a feminized form of "Atlantic" derived from the railroad's name, and the new city began its aggressive growth as a rail hub connecting the Deep South to the Midwest and East Coast. William Tecumseh Sherman spent sixty-seven days laying siege to Atlanta before the city fell on September 2, 1864; on November 15-16, Union troops burned it systematically — warehouses, train depots, factories, and much of the commercial district — before beginning the March to the Sea. The city chose the Phoenix rising from ashes as its official symbol and rebuilt with extraordinary speed, becoming within a generation the commercial capital of the New South — a rebirth premised on the continued exploitation of Black labor under convict leasing, sharecropping, and Jim Crow. Atlanta today markets itself as "the city too busy to hate," a phrase coined during the civil rights era to distinguish it from Birmingham and Selma, though historians note that Atlanta's racial moderation was often pragmatic negotiation rather than genuine conviction.`,
