    description: `Stone Mountain is the largest bas-relief sculpture in the world — a carving of Confederate President Jefferson Davis, General Robert E. Lee, and General Stonewall Jackson on the face of a 825-foot granite dome — and its history encodes the most important story about Confederate commemoration in America: it was commissioned in 1912 by the United Daughters of the Confederacy as part of the Lost Cause movement, the same year the KKK was revived on the mountain's summit on Thanksgiving night 1915, fourteen days after D.W. Griffith's "Birth of a Nation" premiered in Atlanta to enormous white enthusiasm. The Cherokee called this formation "Standing Man Mountain" — Lone Mountain — and it held spiritual significance for Indigenous people for thousands of years before European settlers arrived and before the Confederacy that now claims it as symbol even existed. The state of Georgia took over the project and completed the carving in 1972, during the height of the civil rights and Black Power movements, a timing that was not coincidental: the monument's completion was itself a political statement about who Georgia believed it was. Martin Luther King Jr. referenced Stone Mountain directly in "I Have a Dream": "Let freedom ring from Stone Mountain of Georgia" — a deliberate provocation, demanding that the mountain of white supremacist commemoration become a site of liberation. An ongoing political and legal battle continues over whether and how the carving can be altered or the monument's framing can be changed, with Black Georgians and civil rights organizations arguing that the mountain's history of KKK rallies makes its celebration as a family amusement park an ongoing act of erasure.`,
