    description: `Maynard Holbrook Jackson Jr., elected mayor of Atlanta in 1973, was twenty-five when he ran unsuccessfully against segregationist Herman Talmadge for U.S. Senate and thirty-five when he became the first Black mayor of a major Southern city — two milestones that bracket the most compressed period of political transformation in Southern history. Jackson's most durable achievement was structural rather than rhetorical: he refused to proceed with construction of Hartsfield Atlanta International Airport's new terminal unless minority-owned businesses received 25% of the contracts, a policy called the Minority Business Enterprise program that, when the $400 million project was complete, had created dozens of new Black-owned construction and services companies and served as a national model for affirmative procurement. He served three terms (1974-82, 1990-94), during which Atlanta's Black professional class exploded in size and visibility, creating the conditions for the city's emergence as the destination of choice for Black professionals relocating from Northern cities — the phenomenon journalists would call "the Black Mecca." Jackson died in 2003 at age 65, and his funeral brought together John Lewis, Andrew Young, Bill Clinton, and thousands of Atlantans whose lives he had shaped, a civic grief that testified to the scope of what one elected official can accomplish when he uses institutional power deliberately. His legacy runs through every minority business in Atlanta and through the political infrastructure that made possible the election of subsequent Black mayors and eventually Georgia's first Black senator, Raphael Warnock, in 2021.`,
