    description: `Between July 1979 and May 1981, at least twenty-eight Black children and young adults were killed in Atlanta in a series of murders that the city's Black community experienced not merely as criminal terror but as proof that the lives of Black children were not valued by the city's institutions — police response was slow, investigations were inadequate, and Black parents organized their own neighborhood watch patrols because they did not trust the police to protect their children. The trauma was amplified by the timing: these murders happened in "the Black Mecca," in the city of Maynard Jackson and the Atlanta University Center, in the place Black families had moved from the North in search of safety and opportunity, and the contrast between the city's self-image and the reality of its children's bodies was devastating. Wayne Williams was convicted of two adult murders in 1982 and is widely assumed responsible for most of the child killings, but his guilt has been contested by defense attorneys, journalists, and the families of victims — the Dekalb County reinvestigation opened in 2019 found new DNA evidence that cast doubt on the official narrative. Toni Cade Bambara's novel "Those Bones Are Not My Child," published posthumously in 1999, is the most complete artistic reckoning with the murders and with what the community had to do to survive them — a 700-page act of witness that the literary world has never given it full credit for. The murders left a permanent scar on the generation of Black Atlantans who were children during those years, and the families of the victims have spent four decades demanding truth that has not fully arrived.`,
