    description: `The eight-mile stretch of Buford Highway from Doraville to Chamblee is what the self-organized multicultural economy looks like when left largely to its own devices — Vietnamese, Korean, Chinese, Mexican, Peruvian, Ecuadorian, Ethiopian, Eritrean, Cambodian, and Somali communities have built dense commercial strips here since the 1980s, each occupying storefronts in the aging strip malls that white flight left behind and creating an alternative geography of Atlanta that official tourism has almost entirely ignored. The economic engine is the immigrant small-business ecosystem: international grocery stores like the Buford Highway Farmers Market (one of the largest ethnic grocery markets in the Southeast), pho restaurants, Korean BBQ halls, Oaxacan taquerias, Ethiopian injera houses, and halal butchers clustered within blocks of each other, creating a culinary and commercial landscape that bears no resemblance to the Atlanta of Peach Tree Street and CNN Center. Chinatown Square, International Plaza, and Plaza Fiesta anchor distinct ethnic commercial centers, each serving not just the surrounding community but drawing visitors from across metropolitan Atlanta who understand that this corridor is where authentic food and cultural connection are actually found. The corridor's workforce is disproportionately undocumented, underserved by transit (MARTA does not run along Buford Highway), and politically invisible — a community that generates enormous economic activity while receiving few of the public services that economic activity is supposed to provide. Recent transit and trail planning has begun to address the infrastructure gap, but the corridor's character is a product of its distance from official attention, and there is real anxiety among longtime business owners about what happens when that attention arrives.`,
