    city: `Miami`,
    state: `FL`,
    address: `770 NE 125th St, North Miami, FL 33161`,
    description: `The Museum of Contemporary Art North Miami, founded in 1981 and housed in a distinctive 1996 building designed by Charles Gwathmey, is the primary contemporary art institution serving Miami-Dade's diverse north communities — a museum that has consistently collected and exhibited work by Haitian, Caribbean, African American, and Latin American artists at a time when the mainstream Miami art world was oriented primarily toward the international art market of Art Basel and PAMM. MOCA's collection includes a significant body of work by Haitian artists — Edouard Duval-Carrié, Mario Benjamin, and Didier William among them — collected with the understanding that North Miami's demographic is the Haitian community and that the museum's institutional mission includes serving and representing that community's aesthetic tradition. The museum's jazz series in the outdoor courtyard, running since 1981, has been one of North Miami's primary cultural institutions for over forty years — a free outdoor concert series that draws diverse audiences from across Miami-Dade and that has featured jazz, Afro-Cuban, and Caribbean music traditions that reflect the community's cultural geography. MOCA's financial challenges — the museum has faced budget crises, leadership changes, and questions about its sustainability — reflect the broader difficulty of maintaining a cultural institution with serious curatorial ambitions in a city whose art world is increasingly dominated by private wealth and international commercial interests. The museum's continued operation in North Miami is an act of cultural commitment to communities that the global art world tends to overlook, and its collection of Haitian and Caribbean art is a legacy that any larger institution would value.`,
